//? Title: Maximum clique by colouring bounds
//? Subtitle: Actual greedy classes, DFS decisions and the original seeded density study
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Finding a largest mutually adjacent vertex set is an exponential search problem
in general. A proper colouring of the remaining candidates bounds how many more
vertices can enter a clique. We expose datamunge’s actual greedy-colouring
branch-and-bound search, retain the original graph and forty-vertex density
experiment, and distinguish completed exact results from explicitly limited
search witnesses and bounds.
)

\toc

> Why a colouring bounds the search

Maintain an active clique R and candidates P adjacent to every member of R. Any
child adds one candidate v and intersects the remaining prefix with its
neighbours, preserving the clique/common-neighbour invariant. Greedy first-fit
colouring partitions P into independent classes. A clique contains at most one
vertex from each class, so k classes give the valid upper bound |R|+k, even when
the colouring uses more than the minimum number of colours.

The actual producer groups vertices by increasing colour, then branches in
reverse group order. At a current vertex with colour c, every unprocessed prefix
vertex has colour at most c. If |R|+c cannot exceed the best clique already
found, prune the complete remaining frame. Otherwise schedule a child using that
vertex and its actual adjacent prefix. Leaves replace best only when strictly
larger. Induction over the exhaustive/pruned search proves exactness when every
frame is completed; equal-size alternatives need not be enumerated.

The native algorithm retains this fixed candidate/first-fit rule and legacy tie
order. Its public historical name max_clique_dyn does not mean it implements
every degree/order refinement of the published MaxCliqueDyn method. An explicit
frame stack preserves the original recursive continuation order without
consuming a C++ recursive call stack.

> A tiny cycle: colour two, never clique four

A four-cycle has a proper two-colour root assignment, so its maximum clique is
at most two. The actual search finds a two-vertex witness and then cuts off
frames whose bound can no longer improve it. The diagram reads the actual root
colour count, first branch and completed result size; the table lists actual
greedy order/bounds.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/max_clique_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t =
        dm::algorithms::max_clique_trace(4, {{1, 3}, {0, 2}, {1, 3}, {0, 2}});
    auto &root = t.nodes.front();
    std::print("| actual root order position | actual vertex | actual one-based colour bound |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < root.order.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", i, root.order[i], root.colours[i]);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("bound", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual root greedy colours\n{}", root.colours.back())).node("branch", {6, 0}, std::format("Actual first branch vertex\n{}", t.decisions.front().vertex)).node("result", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual completed clique size\n{}", t.analysis.clique.size())).edge("bound", "branch").edge("branch", "result");
    d.save_svg("build/max_clique_tiny.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual root order position | actual vertex | actual one-based colour bound |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 2 | 1 |
| 2 | 1 | 2 |
| 3 | 3 | 2 |
\image(figures/max_clique_tiny.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual root colouring, first branch and completed witness size on a cycle.)
\alttext(A proper two-colour candidate partition certifies that no clique can contain three or four cycle vertices.)

> Retain the original eight-vertex graph

The original listed fixture contains a4-clique0,1,2,3, a triangle4,5,6, a
bridge2–4 and a pendant7 listed only as7→6. The canonical model validates every
neighbour first, ignores self entries and takes the symmetric union of listed
edges. Thus the pendant is undirected even though its original list entry is
asymmetric. Duplicate listed edges do not create parallel edges.

The diagram uses the native normalization helper, so it displays the actual
graph solved rather than losing an asymmetrically listed edge by filtering only
u<v in the raw input. Highlighted membership is the actual completed maximum
clique. A strict first-maximum rule selects one witness and does not promise a
lexicographically smallest maximum clique in arbitrary tie cases.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/max_clique_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto input = dm::algorithms::max_clique_demo_graph();
    auto t = dm::algorithms::max_clique_trace(8, input);
    auto graph = dm::algorithms::max_clique_normalize(8, input);
    std::print("| actual vertex | in actual completed maximum clique | actual normalized degree |\n| ---: | " "--- | ---: |\n");
    const std::array<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint, 8> positions{
        {{0, 4}, {6, 4}, {6, 0}, {0, 0}, {12, 0}, {18, 4}, {18, -4}, {24, -4}}
    };
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
        bool selected =
            std::find(t.analysis.clique.begin(), t.analysis.clique.end(), i) !=
            t.analysis.clique.end();
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", i, selected, graph[i].size());
        dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
        if (selected)
            style.fill = {220, 252, 231};
        d.node(std::to_string(i), positions[i],
               std::format("Actual vertex {}\nselected {}", i, selected), style);
    }
    for (int u = 0; u < 8; ++u)
        for (int v : graph[u])
            if (u < v)
                d.link(std::to_string(u), std::to_string(v));
    d.save_svg("build/max_clique_original.svg");
}
```
