> Bounded work, complete capture and input guards

All rows and neighbour ids are validated before indexing/capture. Negative n,
row-count mismatch and ids outside0..n−1 throw. Dense N-square geometry and
logical counts reject wrap. Runtime uses a dense adjacency matrix plus explicit
active frames, with O(N^2) storage in the worst case. Search time remains
exponential; colouring is a valid bound, not a polynomial-time solution to
maximum clique.

An optional entered-frame limit is separate from capture. Zero disables the work
cap. Before entering a child frame, an exhausted positive cap stops with
complete=false. The actual best found clique is a valid lower-bound witness; the
actual root greedy-colour count is a valid upper bound. An empty best witness
can be honest when the cap prevents reaching any leaf. Complete results replace
the upper bound by the exact found size. A node cap is not a wall-clock bound,
since one frame’s colouring can itself be expensive.

Trace admission counts N original vertex slots, each listed neighbour, every
actual entered-frame record and its four vector scalar snapshots, every real
colour-class adjacency test record and every leaf/branch/prune event. Default
logical cap is one million; zero disables it. Fields, row headers, capacities,
dense matrix/frame workspace and bytes are excluded. A work stop returns the
complete actual bounded history, including a branch whose child was scheduled
but not entered. Capture failure instead throws without returning a prefix.
Capture size can grow exponentially with visited search history.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/max_clique_budgets.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto graph = dm::algorithms::max_clique_demo_graph();
    std::vector<double> x, lower, upper;
    std::print("| prescribed entered-frame cap | actual entered frames | complete | actual best witness " "size | certified upper bound | captured entries |\n| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: " "|\n");
    for (std::size_t cap : {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32}) {
        auto t = dm::algorithms::max_clique_trace(8, graph, cap);
        auto &a = t.analysis;
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", cap, a.work.nodes,
                   a.complete, a.clique.size(), a.upper_bound,
                   t.captured_entries);
        x.push_back(cap);
        lower.push_back(a.clique.size());
        upper.push_back(a.upper_bound);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, lower, "actual best size", {37, 99, 235});
    p.line(x, upper, "valid upper bound", {220, 38, 38});
    p.x_label("prescribed actual entered-frame cap").y_label(
        "actual lower witness or certified upper size").title(
        "Original graph: actual limited search bounds").save_svg(
        "build/max_clique_budgets.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| prescribed entered-frame cap | actual entered frames | complete | actual best witness size | certified upper bound | captured entries |
| ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1 | false | 0 | 4 | 72 |
| 2 | 2 | false | 0 | 4 | 87 |
| 4 | 4 | false | 0 | 4 | 106 |
| 8 | 5 | true | 4 | 4 | 115 |
| 16 | 5 | true | 4 | 4 | 115 |
| 32 | 5 | true | 4 | 4 | 115 |
\image(figures/max_clique_budgets.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Actual work-limited witnesses and valid bounds.)
\alttext(Incomplete search is labelled explicitly; a capture limit is a different exception-producing control.)

> Verification and references

Native tests exhaust all1100 simple graphs through five vertices against
independent subset enumeration. They reconstruct every actual greedy
class/check, inspect node vectors, replay branch intersections and strict leaf
updates, and verify every bound cutoff and capture count. Original
clique0,1,2,3, asymmetric/self/duplicate input semantics, all work budgets
around completion, exact capture admission, ownership and complete
malformed-input guards are covered. The original density graph stream is
reproduced independently and every returned clique is checked. Existing
graph-hard regression tests remain. The shared ten-report batch passes 71 CTest
cases, 29 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan cases, 130 compiled and executed
public examples, 598/598 scoped documentation definitions and ten representative
hovers. Independent Python arbitrary-precision, hashlib, sorted-window, RNG,
polynomial, graph and full-assignment references verify 224,229 actual records.
Frozen comparisons preserve ordinary legacy outputs, including selected clique
identities, original density graphs and memetic partitions. Twelve checkout
headers and twelve identically installed headers compile independently; an
installed-header native consumer links and runs. All four complete programs and
all four native figures pass execution and visual review, and HTML/Markdown
exports preserve computed results and identical image bytes. Receipts:
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-224-233/`.

The algorithm uses the standard proper-colouring upper-bound principle, proved
above. Published MaxCliqueDyn implementations include additional ordering
refinements; the report deliberately describes this retained native fixed-order
variant and its actual observations rather than attributing unimplemented
refinements or broad performance claims to it.

Konc and Janezic (2007), *An improved branch and bound algorithm for the maximum
clique problem*, MATCH58,569–590,
[publisher paper|https://match.pmf.kg.ac.rs/electronic_versions/Match58/n3/match58n3_569-590.pdf].
Its sections2.2–2.3 describe the basic search and first-fit colour grouping;
later improved ordering/dynamic bounds remain outside this retained native
variant.
